Data Manipulation Language (DML)
1. Definition —

DML (Data Manipulation Language) is a part of SQL used to insert, modify, and delete data stored in database tables.

DML works with the data inside the table, rather than changing the structure of the table.

The commonly used DML commands are:

INSERT – adds new records
UPDATE – modifies existing records
DELETE – removes records

Some database systems also provide commands such as REPLACE and MERGE for specific purposes.

2. What is DML?

Imagine that we have an Employees table.

Employee_ID	Name	Department	Salary
101	Rahul	IT	50000
102	Priya	HR	45000
103	Amit	Sales	40000

The table already exists.

Now suppose we want to:

Add a new employee
Change an employee's salary
Remove an employee

We don't need to create a new table.

We need to manipulate the existing data.

This is where DML is used.

3. DML Commands
Command	Purpose
INSERT	Adds new data
UPDATE	Changes existing data
DELETE	Removes existing data
REPLACE	Replaces/inserts a row in supported databases such as MySQL
MERGE	Performs insert/update logic based on matching conditions; syntax varies by database

Important: DML syntax and behavior can differ between MySQL, PostgreSQL, SQL Server, and Oracle. The examples below mainly use MySQL-style SQL where applicable.

4. INSERT Command
What is INSERT?

The INSERT command is used to add new records into a table.

Syntax
INSERT INTO table_name
(column1, column2, column3)
VALUES
(value1, value2, value3);
Example
INSERT INTO Employees
(Employee_ID, Name, Department, Salary)
VALUES
(104, 'Neha', 'IT', 55000);

Now the table becomes:

Employee_ID	Name	Department	Salary
101	Rahul	IT	50000
102	Priya	HR	45000
103	Amit	Sales	40000
104	Neha	IT	55000
Why specify column names?

Writing column names makes the query clearer and safer.

INSERT INTO Employees
(Employee_ID, Name, Department, Salary)
VALUES
(104, 'Neha', 'IT', 55000);

is generally better than:

INSERT INTO Employees
VALUES
(104, 'Neha', 'IT', 55000);

because the first version clearly tells the database which value belongs to which column.

5. INSERT Multiple Records

We can insert multiple records using a single INSERT statement.

Syntax
INSERT INTO table_name
(column1, column2, column3)
VALUES
(value1, value2, value3),
(value4, value5, value6),
(value7, value8, value9);
Example
INSERT INTO Employees
(Employee_ID, Name, Department, Salary)
VALUES
(105, 'Kiran', 'Finance', 60000),
(106, 'Sneha', 'IT', 58000),
(107, 'Rohit', 'Sales', 42000);

This is useful when we need to add several records at once.

6. UPDATE Command
What is UPDATE?

The UPDATE command is used to modify existing records in a table.

Syntax
UPDATE table_name
SET column_name = new_value
WHERE condition;
Example

Suppose Rahul's salary increases from ₹50,000 to ₹60,000.

UPDATE Employees
SET Salary = 60000
WHERE Employee_ID = 101;

Only Rahul's salary will be changed.

Updating Multiple Columns

We can update more than one column in the same query.

UPDATE Employees
SET Department = 'Finance',
    Salary = 65000
WHERE Employee_ID = 101;

Now both the department and salary are changed.

⚠️ Important: UPDATE without WHERE

Be very careful with this:

UPDATE Employees
SET Salary = 60000;

Because there is no WHERE condition, all employees' salaries will be changed to 60,000.

This is one of the most common mistakes beginners make.

7. DELETE Command
What is DELETE?

The DELETE command is used to remove existing records from a table.

Syntax
DELETE FROM table_name
WHERE condition;
Example

Suppose we want to remove employee 103:

DELETE FROM Employees
WHERE Employee_ID = 103;

The record with Employee_ID = 103 will be deleted.

⚠️ DELETE without WHERE
DELETE FROM Employees;

This removes all records from the table.

The table itself remains available, but its rows are deleted.

So always check your WHERE condition before executing a DELETE.

8. REPLACE Command — MySQL

REPLACE is a MySQL-specific command used to insert a new row or replace an existing row when a PRIMARY KEY or UNIQUE key conflict occurs.

Syntax
REPLACE INTO table_name
(column1, column2, column3)
VALUES
(value1, value2, value3);
Example

Suppose Employee_ID is the primary key.

REPLACE INTO Employees
(Employee_ID, Name, Department, Salary)
VALUES
(101, 'Rahul', 'Finance', 70000);

If employee 101 already exists and the primary key conflicts, MySQL replaces that existing row with the new row.

Important: REPLACE is not the same as a normal UPDATE. It can effectively remove the old row and insert the new row, which may have implications for related data and triggers.

9. MERGE Command

MERGE is used to combine INSERT and UPDATE logic based on whether a matching record already exists.

It is commonly associated with databases such as SQL Server and Oracle, although the exact syntax and support vary by database.

For example, suppose a company receives updated employee data from another system.

If the employee already exists → update the record.

If the employee does not exist → insert a new record.

This type of operation is commonly called an upsert.

Conceptual Example
MERGE Employee_Target AS target
USING Employee_Source AS source
ON target.Employee_ID = source.Employee_ID

WHEN MATCHED THEN
    UPDATE SET target.Salary = source.Salary

WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN
    INSERT (Employee_ID, Name, Salary)
    VALUES (source.Employee_ID, source.Name, source.Salary);

The exact MERGE syntax depends on the database system, so always check the documentation for the database you are using.

10. DML vs DDL
This is an important interview question.

DML	                                          DDL
Works mainly with table data                 	Works with database structure
INSERT	                                      CREATE
UPDATE                                      	ALTER
DELETE                                      	DROP
REPLACE	                                      TRUNCATE
MERGE	                                        RENAME

For example:

INSERT INTO Employees ...

changes the data.

While:

ALTER TABLE Employees ...

changes the table structure.

* TRUNCATE is commonly classified as DDL in many SQL systems, though exact transactional behavior is database-specific.
